    "innovation": "This paper provides a comprehensive review of the Advection-Diffusion-Reaction wildfire model, summarizing recent analytical findings, physical model extensions, and efficient computational strategies to enhance realism and reduce computational costs.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Wildfires",
            "source_entity_type": "Natural_Hazard",
            "relation": "IS_OF_TYPE",
            "target_entity_instance": "Wildfire",
            "target_entity_type": "NaturalHazardType",
            "paragraph": "Wildfires, as a natural disaster, can cause widespread damage to the environment, ecosystems, human residential areas, and local economies. Due to the climate crisis and land-use changes, the frequency and severity of wildfires has significantly increased in the past few decades [10, 28].",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": false
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Advection-Diffusion-Reaction (ADR) model",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_PHYSICAL_FEATURE",
            "target_entity_instance": "continuum approach",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalFeature",
            "paragraph": "The typical ADR model equations are based on the conservation laws of mass and energy applied to a control volume describing the vegetation layer, under a set of simplifying assumptions [21, 29]. A continuum approach is used within the vegetation layer to describe the fuel-air mixture (which is assumed to be incompressible). This allows for the definition of bulk parameters that characterize its effective properties.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Advection-Diffusion-Reaction (ADR) model",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "Newton's cooling law",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "with k the heat conduction coefficient, e the emissivity factor, d the optical path length and & the Stefan-Boltzmann constant, see [4] for details. The second term models the heat loss to the environment, approximated by Newton's cooling law, where h > 0 is the heat exchange coefficient with the environment. The last term represents the combustion process, which is described by the nonlinear function",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Advection-Diffusion-Reaction (ADR) model",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "Arrhenius formula",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "with the Heaviside function H : R \u2192 {0,1}. The function \u03a8\u03c4 is zero for T < T and one for T > T, where the activation temperature Tac, the ignition temperature T, and a pre-exponential factor A > 0 arise from the Arrhenius formula.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": false,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Advection-Diffusion-Reaction (ADR) model",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_PHYSICAL_FEATURE",
            "target_entity_instance": "two-phase porous medium formulation",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalFeature",
            "paragraph": "The wildfire propagation model is formulated over a three-dimensional domain representing the ground surface and a finite height above it. Using a continuum approach, the biomass and surrounding gas (air and combustion products) can be modelled as a two-phase porous medium composed of solid fuel and a gaseous phase. By averaging the quantities over the vertical direction, the problem is reduced to two spatial dimensions defined on the ground surface. At each point in space, bulk properties such as density, specific heat, volume, and mass are defined for both phases using mixture rules [21, 29].",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": false
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "PhyFire model",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "Stefan problem",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "Only a few models explicitly include the effect of fuel moisture. The PhyFire model by Asensio et al. [2] includes sensible and latent water heating using a multi-valued enthalpy-temperature relationship, formulated as a Stefan problem [11, 32].",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Advection-Diffusion-Reaction (ADR) model",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "apparent heat capacity method",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "Finally, Navas-Montilla et al. [21] use the apparent heat capacity method, which represents thermal phase-change effects through an effective, typically piecewise-defined, heat capacity [9, 32]. Three moisture models of increasing complexity are introduced in [21] to describe evaporation and fuel composition. The most comprehensive model captures the key physicochemical mechanisms governing moisture in both live and dead fuels, enabling independent estimation of their specific heat.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": false
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Advection-Diffusion-Reaction (ADR) wildfire model",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "WENO method",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "The combination of the WENO method with the Strong-Stability preserving Runge-Kutta 3 (SSPRK3) has been applied to the ADR wildfire model in [21]. The essentially non-oscillatory property of the WENO method is advantageous when handling under-resolved travelling waves with sharp gradients as those present in the ADR wildfire propagation model.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Advection-Diffusion-Reaction (ADR) wildfire model",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "Strong-Stability preserving Runge-Kutta 3",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "The combination of the WENO method with the Strong-Stability preserving Runge-Kutta 3 (SSPRK3) has been applied to the ADR wildfire model in [21]. The essentially non-oscillatory property of the WENO method is advantageous when handling under-resolved travelling waves with sharp gradients as those present in the ADR wildfire propagation model.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        }
